<div class="container mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
  <%!-- ============================= ABOUT ============================= --%>
  <article :if={@live_action == :about} class="space-y-10">
    <header>
      <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-slate-950 sm:text-4xl">About TempMail Central</h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        TempMail Central is a free temporary email service: open the site, get a working
        inbox at a random address, and let it delete itself when you no longer need it.
        This page explains who runs the service, why it exists, and how it works.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section>
      <h2 class="mb-3 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-950">Who operates the service</h2>
      <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        TempMail Central is an independent, privately operated web service. It is not owned
        by a larger company, and it launched in 2026. The service is free to use and is
        funded by advertising served through Google AdSense; there are no paid plans and no
        sale of user data. The same small team that develops the software also operates the
        servers and answers messages sent through the <.link
          navigate={~p"/contact"}
          class="text-indigo-600 hover:underline"
        >contact page</.link>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="mb-3 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-950">Why we built it</h2>
      <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        Most websites ask for an email address before letting you do anything, and every
        address you hand out becomes a permanent record that can be leaked, sold, or
        spammed. For a one-time download or a trial you will never revisit, a real address
        is more than the situation deserves. A disposable inbox solves that: the sender can
        deliver their verification message, and ten minutes later the address no longer
        exists. Our job is to make that exchange fast, reliable, and honest about its
        limits — including <.link
          navigate={~p"/blog/when-not-to-use-disposable-email"}
          class="text-indigo-600 hover:underline"
        >when you should not use disposable email at all</.link>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="mb-3 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-950">How it works</h2>
      <ol class="list-decimal space-y-3 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        <li>
          When you open the homepage, the app generates a random address — ten lowercase
          letters and digits drawn from a cryptographically secure random source — on one of
          our rotating domains. Operational prefixes such as admin@ or support@ are reserved
          and never issued.
        </li>
        <li>
          The inbox lives in Redis, an in-memory store, with a time-to-live of 10 minutes by
          default. You can extend it to 1 hour or 24 hours without an account, or up to 30
          days when signed in.
        </li>
        <li>
          Incoming mail arrives at our Postfix mail server, is parsed, and is pushed to your
          open browser tab over a WebSocket — messages appear in real time without
          refreshing. Attachments can be viewed and downloaded. Each inbox holds its most
          recent 100 messages.
        </li>
        <li>
          When the timer runs out, the address and every message in it are deleted. Expired
          temporary mail is not archived or backed up anywhere;
          <.link
            navigate={~p"/blog/how-temporary-inbox-deletion-works"}
            class="text-indigo-600 hover:underline"
          >
            this post explains the deletion mechanics
          </.link>
          in detail.
        </li>
        <li>
          With a free account you can save an address as a permanent mailbox (kept until you
          delete it) and connect up to five of your own domains for receiving.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="mb-3 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-950">What the service is — and is not</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full border-collapse text-left text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-slate-200">
              <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-slate-900">Good fit</th>
              <th class="py-3 font-semibold text-slate-900">Wrong tool</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-slate-600">
            <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">One-time signups, downloads, and verifications</td>
              <td class="py-3">Banking, government, healthcare, or employer accounts</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Testing signup and transactional email flows</td>
              <td class="py-3">Anything you may need to recover with a password reset later</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Keeping marketing lists away from your real inbox</td>
              <td class="py-3">Receiving confidential or sensitive documents</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Short-lived, receive-only correspondence</td>
              <td class="py-3">Sending mail — addresses here are receive-only by design</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        One honest caveat worth repeating: while a temporary inbox is alive, anyone who
        knows its exact address can open it. The protection comes from the address being
        one of 3.6 quadrillion possibilities and from its short lifetime — not from a
        password. Permanent mailboxes, by contrast, are private to your account.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="mb-3 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-950">Support</h2>
      <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        The fastest way to reach us is the <.link
          navigate={~p"/contact"}
          class="text-indigo-600 hover:underline"
        >contact form</.link>, which lands directly in the operators' dashboard. You can also
        email
        <a href="mailto:contact@tempmailcentral.com" class="text-indigo-600 hover:underline">
          contact@tempmailcentral.com
        </a>. We read every message and usually reply within a few days. For questions about
        data handling, see the <.link navigate={~p"/privacy"} class="text-indigo-600 hover:underline">Privacy Policy</.link>;
        for the rules of the road, see the
        <.link navigate={~p"/terms"} class="text-indigo-600 hover:underline">Terms of Service</.link>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>

  <%!-- ============================ PRIVACY ============================ --%>
  <article :if={@live_action == :privacy} class="space-y-10">
    <header>
      <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-slate-950 sm:text-4xl">Privacy Policy</h1>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-400">Last updated: August 10, 2026</p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        This policy describes what TempMail Central collects, why, how long it is kept, and
        how to get rid of it. It is written to match what the software actually does.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section>
      <h2 class="mb-3 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-950">The short version</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        <li>Temporary inboxes are held in memory and are permanently deleted when they expire.</li>
        <li>You can use the core service without creating an account or telling us who you are.</li>
        <li>We do not sell personal data.</li>
        <li>
          The service is funded by Google AdSense, which uses cookies; you can control ad
          personalization through Google's own settings.
        </li>
        <li>Server access logs are kept for 14 days for security, then deleted.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="mb-3 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-950">Data we handle when you use a temporary inbox</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        <li>
          <strong>The generated address and its messages.</strong> These are stored in Redis, an
          in-memory database, with a time-to-live. When the timer lapses, the address and all
          messages — including attachments — are deleted and cannot be recovered. Expired
          temporary mail is never archived or backed up.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A local copy of your active address.</strong> Your browser's local storage
          remembers your current temporary address so a page reload can restore the inbox.
          This stays on your device; clearing site data removes it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Web server logs.</strong> Like almost every website, our web server records
          request logs (IP address, user agent, URL, timestamp) for security monitoring and
          abuse prevention. Logs rotate daily and are deleted after 14 days.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Rate-limit counters.</strong> To keep automated abuse in check, request
          counters keyed by IP address are held briefly in application memory and expire on
          their own.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Aggregate statistics.</strong> We keep daily totals — how many addresses were
          generated and how many messages were received — with no link to any visitor. These
          numbers contain no personal data and are kept indefinitely.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        We do not run third-party analytics trackers, and we do not build profiles of
        individual visitors.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="mb-3 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-950">Data we handle if you create an account</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        <li>
          <strong>Sign-in details.</strong> Your email address and a salted hash of your
          password (we never store the password itself), or — if you use Google sign-in — the
          email address Google shares with us.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A durability check at registration.</strong> Because accounts exist to keep
          mail, the address you register with is checked against a disposable-address
          detection service; known throwaway addresses are declined.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your mailboxes and their mail.</strong> Permanent mailboxes, the messages in
          them, and any custom domains you connect are stored in our database until you
          delete them.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Temporary-address history.</strong> When you extend or save a temporary
          address while signed in, we record the address and its expiry so your dashboard can
          show it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Administrative audit log.</strong> Actions taken by service administrators
          (for example, banning an account) are logged for accountability.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="mb-3 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-950">Cookies and local storage</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        <li>
          <strong>Essential session cookie.</strong> A signed cookie keeps you logged in and
          protects forms against forgery. It is required for the site to function.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Preference storage.</strong> Local storage on your device remembers your
          active temporary address and interface state.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Advertising cookies.</strong> Google AdSense sets third-party cookies and
          similar identifiers to serve and measure ads, which may include personalized ads
          depending on your region and consent choices. See
          <a
            href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites"
            rel="noopener nofollow"
            class="text-indigo-600 hover:underline"
          >
            how Google uses information from partner sites
          </a>
          and manage preferences at
          <a
            href="https://adssettings.google.com"
            rel="noopener nofollow"
            class="text-indigo-600 hover:underline"
          >
            Google Ads Settings
          </a>. Where required by law, a consent prompt is shown before advertising cookies
          are used.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="mb-3 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-950">Advertising</h2>
      <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        TempMail Central is free because it shows ads through Google AdSense. Google acts as
        an independent controller of the data its advertising products process. We do not
        pass Google the contents of any inbox, and ad code runs on public pages — not inside
        your mail. We do not sell personal data to anyone.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="mb-3 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-950">How long data is kept</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full border-collapse text-left text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-slate-200">
              <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-slate-900">Data</th>
              <th class="py-3 font-semibold text-slate-900">Retention</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-slate-600">
            <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Temporary inbox and its messages</td>
              <td class="py-3">
                10 minutes by default; extendable to 1 hour or 24 hours (up to 30 days for
                signed-in users). Deleted permanently at expiry.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Permanent mailboxes and their mail (accounts)</td>
              <td class="py-3">Until you delete the mailbox or your account</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Account details</td>
              <td class="py-3">Until you delete your account</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Contact form messages</td>
              <td class="py-3">As long as needed to resolve your request, at most 12 months</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Web server access logs</td>
              <td class="py-3">14 days</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Aggregate daily counters (no personal data)</td>
              <td class="py-3">Indefinitely</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="mb-3 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-950">Who can see a temporary inbox</h2>
      <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        Temporary inboxes are not password-protected: while one is active, anyone who knows
        the exact address can open it. Addresses are drawn from roughly 3.6 quadrillion
        combinations and live for minutes, which makes guessing impractical — but you should
        still never direct sensitive information to a temporary address. Permanent mailboxes
        are different: they are tied to your account and visible only to you (and to us, to
        the extent required to operate and maintain the service).
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="mb-3 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-950">Sharing with third parties</h2>
      <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        We do not sell, rent, or trade personal data. Data leaves our systems only in these
        cases: Google processes advertising data as described above and handles sign-in if
        you choose "Sign in with Google"; the address you register with is checked against a
        disposable-address detection service; and we may disclose information if a valid
        legal order requires it or to protect the service from abuse. There are no other
        recipients.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="mb-3 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-950">Deleting data and your rights</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        <li>Temporary inboxes delete themselves; you can also delete individual messages or generate a new address at any time.</li>
        <li>
          Signed-in users can delete individual mailboxes, all mailboxes, or the entire
          account from the dashboard settings; deletion is immediate and permanent.
        </li>
        <li>
          Depending on where you live, you may have legal rights to access, correct, export,
          or erase personal data we hold about you. Send requests through the
          <.link navigate={~p"/contact"} class="text-indigo-600 hover:underline">contact page</.link>
          with the topic "Privacy or data request" and we will respond.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="mb-3 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-950">Children</h2>
      <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        The service is not directed to children under 13, and we do not knowingly collect
        personal data from them.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="mb-3 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-950">Changes and contact</h2>
      <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        If this policy changes materially, the date at the top will be updated and the new
        version published here. Questions about privacy can be sent through the
        <.link navigate={~p"/contact"} class="text-indigo-600 hover:underline">contact form</.link>
        or to
        <a href="mailto:contact@tempmailcentral.com" class="text-indigo-600 hover:underline">
          contact@tempmailcentral.com
        </a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>

  <%!-- ============================= TERMS ============================= --%>
  <article :if={@live_action == :terms} class="space-y-10">
    <header>
      <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-slate-950 sm:text-4xl">Terms of Service</h1>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-400">Last updated: August 10, 2026</p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        These terms govern your use of TempMail Central. By using the service you agree to
        them; if you do not agree, please do not use the service. You must be at least 13
        years old.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section>
      <h2 class="mb-3 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-950">1. The service</h2>
      <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        TempMail Central provides disposable, receive-only email inboxes that expire
        automatically — after 10 minutes by default, extendable to 1 hour or 24 hours
        without an account and up to 30 days with one — plus optional free accounts with
        permanent mailboxes and custom domain support. Addresses cannot send mail. The
        service is free and supported by advertising.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="mb-3 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-950">2. Acceptable use</h2>
      <p class="mb-3 leading-relaxed text-slate-600">You agree to use the service only for lawful purposes. In particular, you agree to:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        <li>comply with the laws that apply to you when using the service;</li>
        <li>respect the terms of other websites when using a disposable address with them;</li>
        <li>
          accept the temporary nature of the product: expired mail is unrecoverable, and you
          should not rely on the service for communication you cannot afford to lose;
        </li>
        <li>keep your account credentials to yourself and tell us about any compromise.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="mb-3 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-950">3. Prohibited activity</h2>
      <p class="mb-3 leading-relaxed text-slate-600">You may not use TempMail Central to:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        <li>commit fraud, harassment, stalking, or any other illegal act;</li>
        <li>evade bans, sanctions, or restrictions imposed on you by other services;</li>
        <li>receive material that is unlawful to possess or distribute;</li>
        <li>interfere with the service — probing, scraping at abusive volume, denial-of-service, or attempting to access other users' data;</li>
        <li>claim service-operated addresses (reserved prefixes such as admin@, support@, contact@ on our domains) or impersonate the service;</li>
        <li>resell or misrepresent the service as your own.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        We enforce these rules with rate limits, reserved address prefixes, and account
        bans, and we cooperate with lawful requests from authorities. Abuse can be reported
        through the <.link navigate={~p"/contact"} class="text-indigo-600 hover:underline">contact page</.link>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="mb-3 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-950">4. Accounts</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        <li>
          Registration requires a durable email address; known disposable addresses are
          declined, because accounts exist to keep mail beyond a temporary inbox's lifetime.
        </li>
        <li>You are responsible for activity under your account.</li>
        <li>
          We may suspend or ban accounts that violate these terms. You may delete your
          account at any time from the dashboard, which removes your mailboxes and their
          contents.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="mb-3 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-950">5. Availability and changes</h2>
      <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        We aim for reliable operation but make no availability guarantee. Mail can be
        delayed or lost; third-party websites may reject our domains; features, domains,
        and limits (such as inbox lifetime and message caps) may change without notice. We
        may modify or discontinue the service, in whole or in part, at any time.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="mb-3 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-950">6. Disclaimers</h2>
      <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        The service is provided "as is" and "as available", without warranties of any kind,
        express or implied, including merchantability, fitness for a particular purpose, and
        non-infringement. We do not warrant that the service will be uninterrupted, secure,
        or error-free, or that any message will be delivered or retained.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="mb-3 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-950">7. Limitation of liability</h2>
      <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        To the maximum extent permitted by law, we are not liable for indirect, incidental,
        special, or consequential damages, or for lost data, lost profits, or lost access —
        including mail that expired, was not delivered, or was deleted. For a free service,
        our total aggregate liability is limited to the amount you paid us in the twelve
        months before the claim, which is zero. Nothing in these terms excludes liability
        that cannot be excluded under applicable law.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="mb-3 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-950">8. Termination</h2>
      <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        You can stop using the service at any time; deleting your account removes your data
        as described in the <.link navigate={~p"/privacy"} class="text-indigo-600 hover:underline">Privacy Policy</.link>.
        We may suspend or terminate access — including banning accounts and blocking
        traffic — where these terms are violated or to protect the service and its users.
        Sections 6, 7, and 9 survive termination.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="mb-3 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-950">9. Changes to these terms and contact</h2>
      <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        We may update these terms; material changes will be reflected in the date above, and
        continued use after a change constitutes acceptance. Questions about these terms can
        be sent via the <.link navigate={~p"/contact"} class="text-indigo-600 hover:underline">contact page</.link>
        or to
        <a href="mailto:contact@tempmailcentral.com" class="text-indigo-600 hover:underline">
          contact@tempmailcentral.com
        </a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</div>
